(create such another as I have been. I shall die. I shall no longer feel the) Tj
T* (agonies which now consume me or be the prey of feelings unsatisfied, yet) Tj
T* (unquenched. He is dead who called me into being; and when I shall be no) Tj
T* (more, the very remembrance of us both will speedily vanish. I shall no) Tj
T* (longer see the sun or stars or feel the winds play on my cheeks. Light,) Tj
T* (feeling, and sense will pass away; and in this condition must I find my) Tj
T* (happiness. Some years ago, when the images which this world affords first) Tj
T* (opened upon me, when I felt the cheering warmth of summer and heard the) Tj
T* (rustling of the leaves and the warbling of the birds, and these were all to) Tj
T* (me, I should have wept to die; now it is my only consolation. Polluted by) Tj
T* (crimes and torn by the bitterest remorse, where can I find rest but in) Tj
T* (death?) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (?Farewell! I leave you, and in you the last of humankind whom these) Tj
T* (eyes will ever behold. Farewell, Frankenstein! If thou wert yet alive) Tj
T* (and yet cherished a desire of revenge against me, it would be better) Tj
T* (satiated in my life than in my destruction. But it was not so; thou) Tj
T* (didst seek my extinction, that I might not cause greater wretchedness;) Tj
T* (and if yet, in some mode unknown to me, thou hadst not ceased to think) Tj
T* (and feel, thou wouldst not desire against me a vengeance greater than) Tj
T* (that which I feel. Blasted as thou wert, my agony was still superior to) Tj
T* (thine, for the bitter sting of remorse will not cease to rankle in my) Tj
T* (wounds until death shall close them for ever.) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (?But soon,? he cried with sad and solemn enthusiasm, ?I) Tj
T* (shall die, and what I now feel be no longer felt. Soon these burning) Tj
T* (miseries will be extinct. I shall ascend my funeral pile triumphantly and) Tj
T* (exult in the agony of the torturing flames. The light of that conflagration) Tj
T* (will fade away; my ashes will be swept into the sea by the winds. My spirit) Tj
T* (will sleep in peace, or if it thinks, it will not surely think thus.) Tj
T* (Farewell.?) Tj
T* () Tj
T* (He sprang from the cabin-window as he said this, upon the ice raft) Tj
T* (which lay close to the vessel. He was soon borne away by the waves and) Tj
